1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,960
Endometriosis, the pelvic pain that doesn't stop when your period does, isn't just something you have to live with — the fats in your diet can shift your risk of it.

2
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:20,160
About one in ten women of reproductive age has endometriosis, tissue that acts like the uterine lining but grows outside it, on the ovaries, fallopian tubes, or pelvic lining.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:28,400
Every month, that misplaced tissue still responds to estrogen, which triggers bleeding, inflammation, and scar tissue where it doesn't belong.

4
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:38,800
Unlike typical menstrual cramps, this pain often continues before and after your period, radiates to your lower back or thighs, and can show up during sex or bowel movements.

5
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:48,960
Roughly forty percent of women with endometriosis experience infertility, and among women being treated for infertility, twenty-five to fifty percent turn out to have the condition.

6
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:54,960
A twelve-year Harvard study following over one hundred thousand nurses found two specific fats that move the needle.

7
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:05,200
First, trans fat raises risk by forty-eight percent — the biggest sources were fried foods eaten outside the home, especially french fries, along with margarine and crackers.

8
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,960
Second, long-chain omega-3 fatty acids cut risk.

9
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:15,680
Women with the highest omega-3 intake were twenty-two percent less likely to be diagnosed than those with the lowest intake.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,760
If your pain lingers well past your period and shows up during sex or bowel movements, don't just wait it out — talk to a gynecologist.

11
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:29,680
Cutting back on fried food and adding more fatty fish like salmon can support your treatment plan too.
